A||||19||fldevel||What causes these things to burst into flames ??||||1||
Z||000000||fldevel||07-06-2000||12:13 PM||||ive heard alot of  stories about the fiero bursting into flames.  i have never personaly seen this but was wandering what exactly caused the fiero to go up ?? wasent it generly with the 4 cyl models ? ive heard alot of stuff about it but most fail to tell me what caused the car to do this.. thanks <P>---------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||bHooper||07-06-2000||12:24 PM||||yes it was the 4-bangers.<P>generally, the fire was caused by a 4 cyl car being owned by someone that didnt apreciate, or understand their car... a person that thought they were driving a deathtrap, firebox.<P>so be <B>very</B> carefull what you say or do around the car!... it is listening to every word you say about it!<P>ex:  in '96, i cursed my car for not wanting to start (one day), then several days later... the tranny broke!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  and i have a GT!<P>just keep that in mind...<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000002||Deer_Slayer||07-06-2000||12:27 PM||||From what I've heard, the fire problem occured with the 4 cylinders in the 84 models.  The reasons being improper placement of engine components, low oil levels, and driver irresponsibility in care for the engines.  Since then, all the cars were recalled, and the problems corrected.  The 84 4-cylinders were NOT the Tech 4's.  The Tech 4 is the revised engine which came out in 85.  <P>    I have a question for anyone also though.  I heard that the 6 cylinders were notorious for catching fire due to improper heat shields.  The engine's heat was responsible for melting the fuel lines.  Is this true?      ||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||07-06-2000||12:34 PM||||that's the problem with models Deer_Slayer, personally, I think it was the glue - that model cement is highly flammable........oh, you meant the <I>full-size</I> cars!! Generally It was my understanding that it was the early 4-cyl due to the fact that "the factory that manufactured the Fiero engine was shipped faulty connecting rods which combined with the fact that the Fiero 2.5L engine ran a quart low on oil already, made for a fire starting recipe." (reprinted from 'Fiero History' courtesy of Golden Gate Fieros  <A HREF="http://www.goldengatefieros.org" TARGET=_blank>www.goldengatefieros.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||07-06-2000||03:56 PM||||I have an 84' 4 cylinder!  WHat precautions should I take?<P>I always make sure the oil is good and high, what else?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||07-06-2000||04:36 PM||||The problem was mainly in 84, because the duke's only held 3 quarts of oil when they came out (pontiac started using a bigger oil filter to get an extra quart of capacity), and because 20% of the connecting rods that were used were faulty.  <P>The mid-engine design was a factor, too.  If the Fiero put a rod through the block, oil was much more likely to spew onto the catalytic converter and burst into flames.  if a citation or one of the many other cars to use the duke threw a rod, it just spewed oil all over the road.  <P>The plastic body panels are a spectacular sight when they go up in flames, so I've heard..<P>Deer_slayer, I've never heard that... I think the engine would stop running long before it ever got hot enough to melt metal fuel lines...<P>Homicidal -- If the cars survived this long I don't think you have much to worry about  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Just keep that oil at a good level and make sure the cooling system is in good condition.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Sage||07-06-2000||05:15 PM||||Just a footnote here...had a ragged out 86gt catch fire.  Had nothing to do with melting fuel lines.  Was due to shoddy workmanship by some mechanic in the cars past.  He installed cheap rubber,(I think it might have been vacuum line!) from the metal trans lines coming out of the tranny. I concluded this as I was the one who did the repair work, AFTER the fire!  The fluid and age created pin holes in the rubber, and when they got big enough it shot a stream of tranny fluid directly onto the down exhaust pipe, which you know gets pretty hot!  The fluid caught fire, and tried to spread.  We did get it stopped before any real major damage, but was still a pain in the a$$ to replace all the burned up stuff, and the deck lid should really be replaced to be done right.  But as I said, this is a beater.  Does have good body on it and very good space frame.  Will be a candidate for custom work, or for sale!  <P>     Simply put, might pay to check your tranny lines once in a while, and make sure they are in good condition.  After all, the newest Fiero on the road today is 12 years old.  What's that rule about changing belts and hoses? All rubber breaks down sooner or later.  Steel braided lines with a shield, should be fairly good insurance, I would think.<P>Have a good one!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 07-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||tgowens||07-06-2000||08:31 PM||||oh, I just figured out the answer to What causes these things to burst into flames ?? -- fire!! (seriously, the early 4-cyl that had the probs have all either burned up or retired to the bone yards by now -- honestly I wouldn't worry about yours at all -- I am sure it has had the recalls done and maintenance has been performed on it over the years and it has survived -- my guess is that it is past the point of danger by now........enjoy driving it (and working on it) as we all do......these are great little cars!!<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 07-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||jlruiz67||07-06-2000||08:44 PM||||Hello all<BR>   I was the proud owner of an 84 Fiero that had a bit of a fire problem. It melted all the way. I was a good owner and the problem was the Pontiac product that was inferior and later recalled. I know that there where problems with bad maintenance and car from owners but I did take care of my baby and it burned. As I was told at the time there were a few thing wrong. From connecting rods, lack of proper heat shielding, and if you notice there is a fuel hose that runs close to the firewall that was rubbing and after a while with the heat and stress it gave out and there is the fire in my case. I believe that those problems have been taken care of and now if it was to happen it could be poor owner maintenance.<P><BR>Now I'm the proud owner of a 87 GT with 200,000 miles  I drive exitment.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by jlruiz67 (edited 07-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||07-07-2000||02:05 AM||||I had always thought that it was hysteria spawning from the mothers of teenage girls everywhere,and that it never indeed happened any more on a Fiero than on any other kind of car.Guess I'm wrong again.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Philphine||07-07-2000||02:06 AM||||my indy came with a small fire extinguisher.<P>that inspired a great deal of confidence.||||reg||1
Z||000011||shain||07-07-2000||04:35 AM||||what really caused the fiero to catch on fire...... A PHENOMENOM KNOWN AS SFC OR SPONTAINIOUS FIERO COMBUSTION!!<P>no but seriously, it was near the rear fire wall of the car, where the main wirnig harness juntion was. as was the exuast manifold. what happened waas that oil would leak out of the valve cover, sit on the manifold then splash on to the wiring and inturn burn up the car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>what gm did to cure the problem was put a cover over the manifold.<P>i also heard some things about the wiring of the 86 v6 cathing fire too but i dont exactly know what it was.  anyone??||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ironhorse||07-07-2000||02:20 PM||||Mainly basically what everybody said here most of the fire happened because of poor Pontiac design, stupid don't know nothing about fiero owner not taking care of it, and so on.  My 85 Pont Fiero V6 was on fire before I bought it cuz of the fuel pump wire rubbing on the metal firewall, I fixed that then threw a rod.  Tmw I will be putting the engine into F-15, That a name that my friend gave it.   He and I spent 8 hrs checking all the wires on the wireharness and tape them up.  F-15 has recieved most of the recall the heat sheild and such.  When I looked at it and work with it.. I came to a concluision that it's just a temporary fix or a really silly fix. Basically what I did was threw away all the heat sheld on the engine itself. Keeping the heat sheld that's connected to engine bay.  I'm confident that the way I set the motor up that it will works a lot better then what the pontiac come up with.  The only thing major is now that I need to come up with 100 bucks immeadiately so that I can buy Tom's trunk scoop and mount it on there so that all those hot air have somewhere to go instead of lingering on inside the engine bay.||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||07-07-2000||05:19 PM||||I'm not sure if someone said this, but if you've seen the original TSB, the primary cause was a bad casting of connecting rods for the 2.5L. The rods would break, knock a hole in the block and drop oil onto the cat. Bad combination. <P>The next level was not looking a gauges and not enough oil.<P>Bhooper, You've right. There are times I believe my two Fiero's are conspiring against me. Fix one and the other one breaks. Swear to god......||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pontiaddict||07-07-2000||05:36 PM||||One was the pontiac connecting rods. The other was the valve cover leaks. For the v6's , the 2.8 being very warm was the problem. All of the surviing 4 cyls were fixed, there are still v6's that need to be fixed.<BR> At least they didn't have plastic fuel lines  that caught the interior on fire like MR2s!||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierospeeder||07-07-2000||11:25 PM||||i bought my 84 fiero with the previous owner having the recall work done on the car.  I do notice that the wiring harness is covered up with that reflective aluminum foil, generic name, which is going to the fire wall.  I also have the sticker that says check the maintenance and the recall papers and papers that say GM will do free oil changes for a year.  She kept the papers and the sticker in original condition, and also some newspaper articles from 1990 that said the recall about the fiero.   With my Fiero, im pretty sure i put in 5 quarts of oil, its been a while since the oil change, and i havn't driven the car lately.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Boomtastic||07-08-2000||08:30 AM||||Another thing to point out was that in 1984 - the year with the most fires - the rear air vent was centrally located and made from magnesium. Magnesium, when on fire, flares up when hit with water. That makes it tough to put the fire out!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> It was promptly changed with the 1985 models.||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||07-08-2000||12:45 PM||||The header of the "final" version of the recall document for the 4 cylinder as distributed by GM reads:<BR>&gt;Start header quote<P>Number: 88-C-11<BR>Date: 06/90<P>Subject: Product Safety Campaign-88-C-11 / Lack of Adequate Service or Maintenannce<P>Models Affected: 1984-1988 Pontiac Fiero w/ L4 engines (RPO LR8)<P>&gt;End header quote<P>This recall suplements or replaces previous versions of the recall document, including 84-C-15B and 87-C-11. GM provided copies of this to all dealers. It is also available thru the ALLData service repair database service.<P>Note: there may be other recalls for the 84. The best place to dig for them is an ALLdata disk.<P>ALL dealers are required by U.S. Federal law to comply with this NHTSA recall. It's not up to them. It doesn't expire. ever. Dealers refusing to perform recall work should be reported to your state attourny general and GM.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Boomtastic||07-10-2000||04:39 AM||||Ogre - You're correct when you state that Recalls do not expire, however GM (and most major manufacturers) get around this by simply discontinuing the parts. By law they are required to perform the Recall, but there is no law that states that they have to manufacuture/stock obsolete parts, which is *exactly* what GM does. In my 10 years with GM, I have seen this all too many times ...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||07-10-2000||11:08 PM||||good point. This assumes tho that dealers will even look it up. many won't and that's grounds for a complaint to the state AG.<P>In fiero's case Some parts are available, others aren't. It's a royal pain.||||reg||1
